# CardDemo's batch programs: what Katana's rules say each item and block of steps means (R-066).
#
# For Ghost. Each fact is one line that starts with `inferred`; under it, its readings:
#   evidence  what the program does with the item, and where, in the program's own names
#   rule      the rule that proposed it, and how sure the rule is (its measured precision on
#             a hand-written key bounds it; the key is Opus's reading, not a person's)
#   Fable     a proposal only (R-031): a probability and a reason, never a confirmation. A Fable
#             advisor's (J-204), a session separate from the Fable that wrote R-066; the percent
#             is the advisor's on every line; where the line says "reason in Opus's words" the
#             reason is Opus's, written from the advisor's report (J-209)
#   T-xx      each built test's verdict (research section 34): holds, refutes or cannot tell, and why;
#             a test's word is evidence for your answer, never the answer (R-029)
#   docs      the program's authors (AWS's CardDemo README, the copybook and program comments),
#             quoted where they describe the item, "none" where they do not
#
# Your answer goes on the fact line itself, and the next build reads it. Leave the subject (the
# names after the kind) as it is; each line is answered on its own, APPL-RESULT once a program.
#   confirmed   change `inferred` to `confirmed`, and after the confidence append your name and
#               the date: `| Ghost 2026-10-18`
#   refuted     change `inferred` to `refuted`, append the same, then `| ` and what it is instead
#   could not tell  leave `inferred`, and after the confidence append `|  | could not tell: ` (the
#               name field left empty) and what you would have needed; it counts against the
#               evidence line, not you
# No reading, and no agreement among them, makes a fact confirmed; only your word does (R-029).
#
# Words. An item is a named piece of a record or of working storage. A paragraph is a named block
# of steps; PERFORM runs one as a subroutine and comes back; GO TO jumps without coming back. A
# condition-name (an 88 level) is a name for one or more values of an item. MOVE copies; ADD adds
# into; COMPUTE sets from a formula; an edited field is one laid out for printing ($, commas).
# Kinds, as the facts mean them (docs/INTENT-IR.md): money, an amount of money; enum, an item that,
# whenever the program reads it, holds one of the values its condition-names name (a status code
# with other values besides is not one); flag, a yes-or-no; function, a block of steps that only a
# PERFORM enters and that leaves only at its end, with no GO TO in or out.

# program CBTRN02C
#   docs: its header: "Function : Post the records from daily transaction file." (CBTRN02C.cbl line 5)
#   docs: the job step that runs it: "Process and load daily transaction file and create transaction category balance and update transaction master vsam" (POSTTRAN.jcl line 20)
#   docs: README, its jobs table: "POSTTRAN, CBTRN02C, Transaction processing job"

inferred | enum | APPL-RESULT: APPL-AOK, APPL-EOF | enum.disjoint-88s v2 | 0.9
#   evidence: has 2 named values: APPL-AOK = 0, APPL-EOF = 16 (the program also stores 8, 12, which no condition-name names); set in 0000-DALYTRAN-OPEN, 0100-TRANFILE-OPEN, 0200-XREFFILE-OPEN, 0300-DALYREJS-OPEN and 14 more; tested in 0000-DALYTRAN-OPEN, 0100-TRANFILE-OPEN, 0200-XREFFILE-OPEN, 0300-DALYREJS-OPEN and 14 more
#   rule: enum.disjoint-88s v2, confidence 0.9
#   Fable (proposal, R-031): 40% because the program stores 8 into it before each file operation (MOVE 8 TO APPL-RESULT, lines 237, 255, ...), 0 on success, 12 on any other status, 16 on end of file; only 0 and 16 are named. A result code with four values, two named; as the kind is defined ("holds one of its condition-names' values") it is false. The rule is wrong here as stated; a fact "a status code whose values 0 and 16 are named" would be true. → T-E1 (will report 8 and 12 unnamed), T-E2
#   tests: T-E1 (built, EnumValuesStoredTest: its verdict below), T-E2 (built, EnumIsReadTest: its verdict below); each in docs/research/ibm-options.md sections 33 and 34
#   T-E1 refutes: the program stores 8 (in 0000-DALYTRAN-OPEN, 0100-TRANFILE-OPEN, 0200-XREFFILE-OPEN, 0300-DALYREJS-OPEN and 10 more); stores 12 (in 0000-DALYTRAN-OPEN, 0100-TRANFILE-OPEN, 0200-XREFFILE-OPEN, 0300-DALYREJS-OPEN and 14 more), which no condition-name names
#   T-E2 holds: tested in 0000-DALYTRAN-OPEN, 0100-TRANFILE-OPEN, 0200-XREFFILE-OPEN, 0300-DALYREJS-OPEN and 14 more
#   docs: none

inferred | function | 0000-DALYTRAN-OPEN | function.perform-range v1 | 1
#   evidence: the block 0000-DALYTRAN-OPEN (5 statements: a MOVE, an OPEN, 2 IFs, an EXIT (which does nothing; it marks the end)) is run by PERFORM from the main line (the statements before the first paragraph name) and, when it runs off its end, returns to the statement after that PERFORM; nothing jumps into it or out of it with GO TO
#   rule: function.perform-range v1, confidence 1
#   Fable (proposal, R-031; reason in Opus's words): 98% because a single block wrapping one file operation, entered only by PERFORM, with no GO TO in or out; what remains is the paragraph graph being wrong about an edge, which T-F1 and T-F2 check
#   tests: T-F1 (built, FunctionRangeStructureTest: its verdict below), T-F2 (built, FunctionRangeXrefTest: its verdict below), T-F3 (built, PerformTracesTest: its verdict below); each in docs/research/ibm-options.md sections 33 and 34
#   T-F1 holds: on sir/'s paragraph graph only a PERFORM of the whole block enters it, it leaves only at its end, and the main line does not fall into it
#   T-F2 holds: on cobc's own cross-reference listing every reference to its first paragraph is a PERFORM of the whole block, nothing outside names a paragraph inside, and no GO TO inside names one outside
#   T-F3 holds: the java lane's runs of the proven jobs enter it 1 times, each by a PERFORM of the whole block, and it returns 1 times; control never falls into or out of it
#   docs: none

